  "story": {
    "summary": "Changes how TypeCollector handles bad keys. After reporting a mixed int/string key diagnostic, it now skips that member with `continue` (lines 1008, 1148). It also stops adding a second member with an already-used int or string key, using a ContainsKey guard (lines 1023-1026, 1039-1042, 1163-1166). Previously these paths could throw: `intKey!.Value` was read with a null key, and Dictionary.Add threw on a duplicate key. The change adds analyzer tests for `SuppressSourceGeneration = true` types, a runtime round-trip execution test, and a README section describing which diagnostics apply to suppressed types.",
    "why": "Issue #2234: since 3.1.1 the analyzer stops reporting diagnostics on `[MessagePackObject(SuppressSourceGeneration = true)]` types. The diff's implied root cause is that the analyzer threw while collecting members with bad keys.",
    "touches_beyond_requirement": [
      "README.md gains a new user-facing 'Opting out of source generation' section. It promises which rules do and do not fire for suppressed types.",
      "A new execution test checks runtime serialization of a suppressed type through the dynamic resolver. This is not analyzer behavior.",
      "The crash fixes also change behavior for ordinary, non-suppressed [MessagePackObject] types (see DuplicateKeysAreReportedWithoutAnalyzerFailureForGeneratedTypes)."
    ],
    "confidence_overview": "High that the edited lines remove specific throw paths: a null `intKey!.Value` and a duplicate-key Dictionary.Add. Medium-to-low that this is the actual cause of #2234. No line in the diff mentions SuppressSourceGeneration, and the visible lines do not show why suppressed types were affected and generated types were not. The unchanged code that decides whether suppressed types are analyzed at all is not visible."
  },
